  "rationale": "Fixed by the science lane on 2026-10-30 before any MODIS NDVI or CHIRPS value of this catchment was read. In a Mediterranean winter-rain climate the annual grasses germinate with the first autumn rains, grow slowly through winter, grow fast in spring and reach their peak standing crop before the dry season (UC ANR Publication 8018, Becchetti et al. 2016, which a search summary describes as naming these phases: break of season with the first fall rains, winter growth, rapid spring growth, peak forage production; the publication itself was not read, so the month boundaries are the lane's reading of the phases and not figures taken from it). February to May is therefore the primary window, spanning the end of winter growth to the peak and the start of drying; January to April is the earlier and March to June the later sensitivity window, and March to June is also the first place's primary window, kept so the two places can be compared. October to April is the rainfall window: it runs from the first autumn rains to the end of the main wet season (Gennet et al. 2017, PLoS ONE, describe the northern Diablo Range as a Mediterranean climate with cool wet winters and hot dry summers and do not give the months). The SFPUC Alameda Creek Watershed Rangeland Management Plan (March 2026), for the lands that drain to Calaveras and San Antonio reservoirs, states that the majority of rainfall occurs between October and April with little to no precipitation in the remainder of the year, and records average precipitation of 10 to 20 inches per year; this was read after the window was registered and agrees with it (revision 5). A single rainfall window is registered; no sensitivity rainfall window was chosen."
 },
